A1. Cross this one and the rest will be easier. ~ CROSS
A5. You could get into this with a pair. ~ ARK
A8. Something fat baldheaded men would gladly part with. ~ COMB
A12. Excess baggage for Paul Whiteman. ~ FAT
A13. You will have to fail here to succeed. ~ FAIL
A15. What many an old wreck clings to. ~ REEF
A17. If you don't have room for this, abbreviate it. ~ RM
A19. This is dead from the waist up. ~ BUST
A21. Scotch water. ~ MINERAL
A23. "Take the first turn to the left, go three blocks, turn to the left again, then take the first right-hand turn on the right when you come to a white house, then go four blocks till you come to a garage, then take the left-hand——" ~ DIRECT
A25. If things seem dull, use this. ~ HONE
A26. This is no laughing matter. ~ CRY
A27. The M. D.'s competitor. ~ STORK
A28. A little thing that can help make a gal. ~ PINT
A29. Caesar's next to last word. ~ TU
A30. A screwy worm. ~ ESS
A31. This fellow does a great monkey business. ~ DAGO
A32. Dumb Dora tried to eat this and got a stone bruise. ~ CARAT
A36. The heart of the matter. ~ PITH
A37. A girl's name that begins like a cure for unemployment. ~ JOBYNA
A38. The direction of Heaven. ~ UP
A40. Signs of stilted brains. ~ HIGHBROWS
A42. The beginning and end of 365 days. ~ YR
A43. These make the fiddle bow fiddle. ~ RESINS
A45. You'll never see these birds again. ~ AUKS
A47. How the runner felt after the race. ~ SPENT
A48. Dora would call this a runaway bug. ~ FLEE
A49. All bound round. ~ MID
A51. A best seller. ~ AD
A52. What the coy wrestler got on his girl. ~ HOLD
A53. Here's a hard one for you: where does Mother work every winter? ~ CIDER
A55. This is always up a tree, being all wet. ~ SAP
A57. The mere presence of this man about makes you want to do things. ~ BOSS
A58. Prisoners sometimes use this to get out. ~ PAROLE
A59. Lovers get this way after awhile. ~ AMOROUS
A61. This doesn't amount to much. ~ IOTA
A62. If you get this, you'll be getting a short weight. ~ LB
A63. Jack and this and his brother were the plot of an old story. ~ DORY
A64. Rendezvous for a lot of old skates. ~ RINK
A66. This was opened with a loud bang the other morning. ~ EGG
A68. Smokestack. ~ STEM
A69. A famous soap-maker. ~ LYE
A70. What to do when your motor needs erl. ~ REOIL

D1. These make good deck hands and fortune tellers. ~ CARDS
D2. Preposition. ~ OF
D3. These are very cutting. ~ SABERS
D4. If you get this you won't be able to finish the puzzle. ~ STUCK
D5. This is very stern. ~ AFT
D6. Old-fashioned heater. ~ RA
D7. Women seldom wear this out. ~ KIMONO
D9. The source of gold teeth. ~ ORE
D10. This is due for a rise any day now. ~ MERCURY
D11. Dora thinks this one is hairless. ~ BEAR
D14. What old maids find under the bed. ~ LINT
D16. Some poor fish is bound to bite at this one. ~ FLY
D18. You can put this in your eye. ~ MITE
D20. A chicken crosses this because Rudy Vallee is on the other side. ~ ST
D22. Up and right on the map. ~ NE
D24. "Twas in a garden I found you blushing." ~ ROSE
D25. What ballplayers reach for expertly. ~ HIGHBALLS
D28. This ran right across my garden. ~ PATH
D29. What she keeps on her husband. ~ TABS
D31. Prepares the garden for the neighbors' chickens. ~ DIGS
D32. These aren't ashamed to show their calves. ~ COWS
D33. This is unlimited in Chicago. ~ ANY
D34. A sour gal. ~ TART
D35. Things to pelt girls with. ~ FURS
D36. This is enough to get you dizzy. ~ PINT
D37. Stop me if you've heard this one. ~ JOKE
D39. Large gobs of go. ~ PEP
D40. Female of the deer. ~ HIND
D41. What he did the morning after. ~ RUED
D44. A sailor's home. ~ SEAPORT
D46. There's not much dog to this. ~ FIDO
D48. An antiquated old stiff. ~ FOSSIL
D49. Now you see it, now you don't. ~ MIRAGE
D50. Where the farmer was all the time. ~ DELL
D52. Since this is only a matter of time, you're bound to get it. ~ HOUR
D53. What confectioners do to their wives' wishes. ~ CATER
D54. This one is revolting. ~ REBEL
D55. You can't have much fun in this state. ~ SAD
D56. An andy man. ~ AMOS
D57. Tramp, tramp, tramp. ~ BO
D58. Eyetalian river. ~ PO
D60. If the coast guard meets rum runners comin' thru with this—Bang! ~ RYE
D61. A Mike man. ~ IKE
D65. How to write your initial letters to a big city. ~ NY
D67. A welcome sign to the motorist. ~ GO


Warnings:
Note: The answer key ran in the April 11, 1931 issue (page 31, 'Solution of Last Week's Puzzle'). The harvest found only 12 of its 15 columns and didn't match it; the key here is read from the scan.

